{ "answer": ["leuprolide","lupron","leuprolide acetate"], "clues": ["This drug is used by zoos and wildlife sanctuaries around the world for exotic animal population control. Because it safely and reversibly shuts down the reproductive cycle across species, the St. Louis Zoo's AZA Reproductive Management Center profiles it as a contraceptive tool for captive wildlife. It has been used to manage everything from aggressive behavior in male lions to controlling fertility in migratory birds and marsupials","In 2001, this drug became the center of one of the largest healthcare fraud settlements in U.S. history at the time. Its joint-venture manufacturer, TAP Pharmaceuticals, paid an $875 million criminal and civil settlement to the U.S. Department of Justice. Sales reps had been caught giving free samples of this medication to urologists, who would then inject patients and bill Medicare for the full price. The scheme artificially inflated the drug's popularity and cost taxpayers millions before a whistleblower system brought it down","This is a gonadotropin-releasing hormone agonist. Initially, it stimulates the pituitary gland but with continuous exposure, the pituitary glad shuts down production of LH and FSH. It is used in prostate cancer, endometriosis, central precocious puberty, and uterine fibroids"] },
